WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.356 --> 00:14.066 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference September 17,

00:14.526 --> 00:17.506 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:17.506 --> 00:18.466 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:18.466 --> 00:20.006 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:20.006 --> 00:22.936 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:22.936 --> 00:26.326 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:26.326 --> 00:29.646 align:middle
While the unemployment rate
remains low, it has edged up,

00:29.646 --> 00:31.646 align:middle
job gains have slowed,
and downside risks

00:32.666 --> 00:33.686 align:middle
to employment have risen.

00:33.686 --> 00:35.146 align:middle
At the same time,
inflation has risen recently

00:35.146 --> 00:36.576 align:middle
and remains somewhat elevated.

00:38.526 --> 00:40.696 align:middle
In support of our goals,
and in light of the shift

00:40.696 --> 00:41.866 align:middle
in the balance of risks,

00:42.106 --> 00:44.796 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:44.796 --> 00:47.616 align:middle
to lower our policy interest
rate by ¼ percentage point.

00:48.486 --> 00:51.516 align:middle
We also decided to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

00:52.536 --> 00:54.576 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

00:54.576 --> 00:57.646 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

00:59.086 --> 01:00.896 align:middle
Recent indicators
suggest that growth

01:00.946 --> 01:02.936 align:middle
of economic activity
has moderated.

01:03.586 --> 01:09.326 align:middle
GDP rose at a pace of around
1½ percent in the first half

01:09.326 --> 01:12.376 align:middle
of the year, down from
2.5 percent last year.

01:13.706 --> 01:16.496 align:middle
The moderation in growth
largely reflects a slowdown

01:16.496 --> 01:17.516 align:middle
in consumer spending.

01:18.836 --> 01:21.516 align:middle
In contrast, business
investment in equipment

01:21.516 --> 01:24.256 align:middle
and intangibles has picked
up from last year's pace.

01:25.446 --> 01:27.696 align:middle
Activity in the housing
sector remains weak.

01:29.236 --> 01:30.986 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

01:30.986 --> 01:33.196 align:middle
the median participant
projects GDP

01:33.196 --> 01:37.696 align:middle
to rise 1.6 percent this year
and 1.8 percent next year,

01:38.206 --> 01:40.116 align:middle
a touch stronger than
projected in June.

01:40.116 --> 01:44.696 align:middle
In the labor market, the
unemployment rate edged

01:44.696 --> 01:47.956 align:middle
up to 4.3 percent in August
but remains little changed

01:47.956 --> 01:50.756 align:middle
over the past year at
a relatively low level.

01:52.056 --> 01:54.826 align:middle
Payroll job gains have slowed
significantly to a pace

01:54.896 --> 01:58.446 align:middle
of just 29,000 per month
over the past three months.

01:59.686 --> 02:02.556 align:middle
A good part of the slowing
likely reflects a decline

02:02.556 --> 02:05.966 align:middle
in the growth of the labor
force due to lower immigration

02:06.126 --> 02:08.266 align:middle
and lower labor force
participation.

02:09.596 --> 02:12.526 align:middle
Even so, labor demand has
softened, and the recent pace

02:12.526 --> 02:15.286 align:middle
of job creation appears
to be running below the

02:15.286 --> 02:18.956 align:middle
"breakeven" rate needed to hold
the unemployment rate constant.

02:19.586 --> 02:23.366 align:middle
In addition, wage growth
has continued to moderate,

02:23.496 --> 02:25.236 align:middle
while still outpacing inflation.

02:26.626 --> 02:30.246 align:middle
Overall, the marked slowing in
both the supply of and demand

02:30.246 --> 02:31.986 align:middle
for workers is unusual.

02:32.866 --> 02:36.006 align:middle
In this less dynamic and
somewhat softer labor market,

02:36.526 --> 02:39.056 align:middle
the downside risks to
employment appear to have risen.

02:40.586 --> 02:43.056 align:middle
In our SEP, the median
projection

02:43.056 --> 02:46.816 align:middle
for the unemployment rate is 4.5
percent at the end of this year

02:47.286 --> 02:48.656 align:middle
and edges down thereafter.

02:50.486 --> 02:53.126 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly from its highs

02:53.126 --> 02:56.896 align:middle
in mid-2022 but remains
somewhat elevated relative

02:56.896 --> 02:58.636 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

03:00.456 --> 03:03.406 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

03:03.406 --> 03:04.616 align:middle
and other data indicate

03:04.616 --> 03:07.686 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.7 percent

03:07.686 --> 03:10.736 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in August and that,

03:10.736 --> 03:13.506 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:13.966 --> 03:16.686 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.9 percent.

03:18.186 --> 03:20.406 align:middle
These readings are higher
than earlier in the year,

03:20.506 --> 03:22.426 align:middle
as inflation for
goods has picked up.

03:23.746 --> 03:26.136 align:middle
In contrast, disinflation
appears

03:26.136 --> 03:27.816 align:middle
to be continuing for services.

03:28.876 --> 03:31.786 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have moved up,

03:32.046 --> 03:36.136 align:middle
on balance, over the course of
this year on news about tariffs,

03:36.136 --> 03:39.096 align:middle
as reflected in both market-
and survey-based measures.

03:40.466 --> 03:43.616 align:middle
Beyond the next year or
so, however, most measures

03:43.616 --> 03:46.016 align:middle
of longer-term expectations
remain consistent

03:46.016 --> 03:47.716 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:49.196 --> 03:51.146 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

03:51.296 --> 03:55.866 align:middle
for total PCE inflation is 3.0
percent this year and falls

03:55.866 --> 04:01.456 align:middle
to 2.6 percent in 2026 and
to 2.1 percent in 2027.

04:02.696 --> 04:06.986 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

04:06.986 --> 04:09.576 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

04:09.576 --> 04:10.736 align:middle
for the American people.

04:11.396 --> 04:14.096 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

04:14.096 --> 04:16.316 align:middle
to lower the target range
for the federal funds rate

04:16.746 --> 04:20.626 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point
to 4 to 4¼ percent

04:21.936 --> 04:24.776 align:middle
and to continue reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

04:26.516 --> 04:28.876 align:middle
Changes to government
policies continue to evolve,

04:29.036 --> 04:31.536 align:middle
and their effects on the
economy remain uncertain.

04:32.946 --> 04:35.076 align:middle
Higher tariffs have
begun to push up prices

04:35.076 --> 04:38.096 align:middle
in some categories of goods,
but their overall effects

04:38.096 --> 04:41.076 align:middle
on economic activity and
inflation remain to be seen.

04:42.316 --> 04:44.516 align:middle
A reasonable base case
is that the effects

04:44.516 --> 04:48.206 align:middle
on inflation will be relatively
short lived-a one-time shift

04:48.306 --> 04:49.106 align:middle
in the price level.

04:49.996 --> 04:53.256 align:middle
But it is also possible that
the inflationary effects could

04:53.256 --> 04:56.666 align:middle
instead be more persistent,
and that is a risk

04:56.776 --> 04:58.456 align:middle
to be assessed and managed.

04:59.196 --> 05:01.746 align:middle
Our obligation is to ensure
that a one-time increase

05:01.746 --> 05:04.496 align:middle
in the price level does
not become an ongoing

05:04.496 --> 05:05.346 align:middle
inflation problem.

05:05.536 --> 05:09.786 align:middle
In the near term, risks
to inflation are tilted

05:09.786 --> 05:11.546 align:middle
to the upside and
risks to employment

05:11.666 --> 05:13.976 align:middle
to the downside-a
challenging situation.

05:14.916 --> 05:18.386 align:middle
When our goals are in tension
like this, our framework calls

05:18.386 --> 05:20.946 align:middle
for us to balance both
sides of our dual mandate.

05:22.106 --> 05:24.486 align:middle
With downside risks to
employment having increased,

05:24.836 --> 05:26.416 align:middle
the balance of risks
has shifted.

05:27.576 --> 05:29.906 align:middle
Accordingly, we judged it
appropriate at this meeting

05:29.906 --> 05:33.266 align:middle
to take another step toward
a more neutral policy stance.

05:34.286 --> 05:37.416 align:middle
With today's decision, we remain
well positioned to respond

05:37.416 --> 05:39.956 align:middle
in a timely way to potential
economic developments.

05:40.856 --> 05:43.046 align:middle
We will continue to determine
the appropriate stance

05:43.046 --> 05:45.746 align:middle
of monetary policy based
on the incoming data,

05:46.586 --> 05:48.836 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

05:50.556 --> 05:53.486 align:middle
In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

05:53.486 --> 05:54.956 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

05:54.956 --> 05:57.026 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

05:57.686 --> 05:59.716 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

05:59.716 --> 06:01.886 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario for the economy.

06:03.316 --> 06:05.786 align:middle
The median participant projects
that the appropriate level

06:05.786 --> 06:08.386 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 3.6 percent at the end

06:08.386 --> 06:12.376 align:middle
of this year, 3.4 percent
at the end of 2026,

06:12.706 --> 06:15.916 align:middle
and 3.1 percent at
the end of 2027.

06:16.726 --> 06:20.716 align:middle
This path is ¼ percentage point
lower than projected in June.

06:21.936 --> 06:25.326 align:middle
As is always the case, these
individual forecasts are subject

06:25.326 --> 06:28.676 align:middle
to uncertainty, and they are not
a Committee plan or decision.

06:29.306 --> 06:31.496 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

06:32.036 --> 06:34.916 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

06:34.916 --> 06:37.956 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

06:38.786 --> 06:41.126 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

06:41.456 --> 06:44.286 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

06:44.606 --> 06:47.226 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

06:47.296 --> 06:47.876 align:middle
well anchored.

06:48.996 --> 06:50.236 align:middle
Our success in delivering

06:50.236 --> 06:52.306 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

06:52.936 --> 06:55.886 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

06:55.886 --> 06:57.536 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

06:58.866 --> 07:01.156 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission,

07:01.306 --> 07:03.026 align:middle
and we at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:03.606 --> 07:05.326 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:05.396 --> 07:06.686 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:07.216 --> 07:07.656 align:middle
Thank you.

07:07.656 --> 07:09.326 align:middle
I look forward to
our discussion.

07:09.666 --> 07:13.476 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

07:13.586 --> 07:14.616 align:middle
Great. Thank you.

07:14.826 --> 07:16.856 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

07:16.856 --> 07:21.776 align:middle
As you know, Fed Governor
Stephen Miran has continued-kept

07:21.776 --> 07:23.456 align:middle
his position at the
White House even

07:23.456 --> 07:24.646 align:middle
as he's joined the Fed Board.

07:24.646 --> 07:26.606 align:middle
This is the first
time, I believe,

07:26.606 --> 07:27.846 align:middle
the Fed's Board has had someone

07:27.846 --> 07:30.746 align:middle
with executive-branch
ties in decades.

07:31.346 --> 07:33.616 align:middle
Does this compromise
the Fed's independence

07:33.616 --> 07:34.806 align:middle
from day-to-day politics?

07:34.936 --> 07:38.256 align:middle
And, relatedly, how can you
maintain the public's perception

07:38.256 --> 07:40.466 align:middle
the Fed is politically
independent with this dynamic?

07:40.466 --> 07:40.766 align:middle
Thank you.

07:41.126 --> 07:41.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:42.456 --> 07:45.716 align:middle
So we did welcome a new
Committee member today,

07:45.846 --> 07:48.576 align:middle
as we always do, and the
Committee remains united

07:48.576 --> 07:51.296 align:middle
in pursuing our dual-mandate
goals.

07:52.006 --> 07:55.546 align:middle
We're strongly committed to
maintaining our independence,

07:55.546 --> 07:57.366 align:middle
and beyond that I really
don't have anything to share.

07:58.556 --> 07:58.956 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

07:58.956 --> 08:00.576 align:middle
And then just as a quick
follow-up-I'm sorry.

08:00.656 --> 08:03.096 align:middle
Can you-you and other
Fed officials have spoken

08:03.096 --> 08:07.116 align:middle
extensively about the impact of
tariffs on inflation, although,

08:07.116 --> 08:10.036 align:middle
perhaps, with many companies
appearing to "eat" the tariffs,

08:11.796 --> 08:14.486 align:middle
tariffs may be impacting the
labor market and other parts

08:14.486 --> 08:15.506 align:middle
of the economy instead.

08:16.106 --> 08:18.566 align:middle
Do you see that as a possible
outcome here-that tariffs are

08:18.826 --> 08:20.366 align:middle
the reason we're
seeing some slowing,

08:20.366 --> 08:21.886 align:middle
particularly in the
labor market?

08:21.886 --> 08:23.136 align:middle
Rather than in inflation?

08:23.276 --> 08:24.016 align:middle
Thank you.

08:24.016 --> 08:24.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:25.126 --> 08:26.516 align:middle
You know, it's certainly
possible.

08:26.666 --> 08:29.556 align:middle
You know, we're beginning
to see-we have begun

08:29.556 --> 08:32.056 align:middle
to see [increased]
goods prices showing

08:32.056 --> 08:34.076 align:middle
through into higher inflation.

08:34.076 --> 08:37.516 align:middle
And, actually, the increase in
goods prices accounts for most

08:37.516 --> 08:40.076 align:middle
of the increase in inflation
or, perhaps, all of the increase

08:40.076 --> 08:41.676 align:middle
in inflation over the
course of this year.

08:42.316 --> 08:45.326 align:middle
Those are not very large
effects at this point.

08:45.936 --> 08:49.226 align:middle
And we do expect them
to continue to build

08:49.276 --> 08:52.366 align:middle
over the course of the rest of
the year and into next year.

08:52.886 --> 08:54.336 align:middle
And it's also possible

08:54.436 --> 08:57.646 align:middle
that there may be
effects on employment.

08:58.246 --> 09:00.576 align:middle
But I would say,
if you're looking

09:00.576 --> 09:03.066 align:middle
at why employment is doing what
it's doing, that's much more

09:03.066 --> 09:04.366 align:middle
about the change in immigration.

09:04.676 --> 09:07.996 align:middle
So the supply of workers has,
obviously, come way down.

09:07.996 --> 09:10.726 align:middle
There's very little growth, if
any, in the supply of workers.

09:11.306 --> 09:13.916 align:middle
And at the same time, demand
for workers has also come

09:13.916 --> 09:17.336 align:middle
down quite sharply
and to the point

09:17.336 --> 09:20.766 align:middle
where we see what I've
called a "curious balance."

09:20.826 --> 09:22.546 align:middle
Typically, when we
say things are

09:22.546 --> 09:23.736 align:middle
"in balance," that sounds good.

09:23.736 --> 09:27.006 align:middle
But, in this case, the
balance is because both supply

09:27.006 --> 09:30.586 align:middle
and demand have come down quite
sharply, now demand coming

09:30.586 --> 09:31.616 align:middle
down a little more sharply

09:31.616 --> 09:34.226 align:middle
because we see-we now see the
unemployment rate edging up.

09:34.686 --> 09:37.746 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:38.946 --> 09:42.186 align:middle
Nick Timiraos with the
Wall Street Journal.

09:42.886 --> 09:45.376 align:middle
Chair Powell, do economic
conditions and the balance

09:45.376 --> 09:48.636 align:middle
of risks no longer warrant a
restrictive policy setting?

09:49.126 --> 09:50.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:50.036 --> 09:51.946 align:middle
So I don't think
we can say that.

09:51.946 --> 09:55.716 align:middle
What we can say is this: that
over the course of this year,

09:55.986 --> 09:57.616 align:middle
we've kept our policy

09:57.616 --> 10:00.286 align:middle
at a restrictive level-and
people have different views,

10:00.286 --> 10:02.506 align:middle
but a clearly restrictive
level, I would say.

10:02.506 --> 10:06.336 align:middle
And we were able to do that
over the course of this year

10:06.336 --> 10:08.846 align:middle
because the labor market
was in very solid condition,

10:08.846 --> 10:10.936 align:middle
with strong job creation
and all those things.

10:11.436 --> 10:14.346 align:middle
I think if you go back
to April and now look

10:14.346 --> 10:17.956 align:middle
at the revised job-creation
numbers for May, June, July,

10:18.376 --> 10:21.656 align:middle
and August, you can kind
of-I can no longer say that.

10:21.796 --> 10:24.226 align:middle
So what that means
is that the risks,

10:24.226 --> 10:27.676 align:middle
which-the risks were clearly
tilted toward inflation.

10:27.936 --> 10:30.966 align:middle
I would say they're
moving toward equality.

10:30.966 --> 10:31.706 align:middle
Maybe they're not quite

10:31.706 --> 10:33.226 align:middle
at equality-we don't
need to know that.

10:33.226 --> 10:36.616 align:middle
But we do know that they've
moved meaningfully toward

10:37.246 --> 10:40.336 align:middle
greater equality-the risks
between the two goals-and

10:40.336 --> 10:41.916 align:middle
that suggests that
we should be moving

10:41.916 --> 10:43.056 align:middle
in the direction of neutral.

10:43.056 --> 10:43.886 align:middle
And that's what we did today.

10:43.886 --> 10:44.246 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:45.066 --> 10:47.086 align:middle
Under what circumstances
would a larger

10:47.086 --> 10:49.946 align:middle
than 25 basis points
rate cut be warranted?

10:50.346 --> 10:52.956 align:middle
And how seriously was
that option entertained

10:52.956 --> 10:53.996 align:middle
at your meeting this week?

10:53.996 --> 10:54.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:54.986 --> 10:57.176 align:middle
There wasn't widespread
support at all

10:57.176 --> 10:59.176 align:middle
for a 50 basis point cut today.

10:59.176 --> 11:02.726 align:middle
You know, I think we've
done-we've done very large rate

11:02.726 --> 11:05.786 align:middle
hikes and very large rate
cuts in the last five years,

11:06.266 --> 11:09.696 align:middle
and you tend to do those
at a time when you feel

11:09.696 --> 11:12.136 align:middle
that policy is out
of place and needs

11:12.136 --> 11:14.306 align:middle
to move quickly to a new place.

11:14.306 --> 11:16.556 align:middle
That's not at all what
I feel, certainly now.

11:16.556 --> 11:19.406 align:middle
I feel like our policy has
been doing the right thing

11:19.406 --> 11:20.206 align:middle
so far this year.

11:20.206 --> 11:23.296 align:middle
I think we were right to
wait and see how tariffs

11:23.296 --> 11:25.856 align:middle
and inflation and the
labor market evolved.

11:26.316 --> 11:30.766 align:middle
I think we're now reacting
to the much lower level

11:30.766 --> 11:32.926 align:middle
of job creation and other
evidence of softening

11:32.926 --> 11:34.376 align:middle
in the labor market
and saying, well,

11:34.376 --> 11:37.396 align:middle
those risks are maybe not
fully balanced but moving

11:37.396 --> 11:38.736 align:middle
in the direction of balance now,

11:38.736 --> 11:40.646 align:middle
and so that warrants
a change in policy.

11:41.996 --> 11:43.616 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

11:44.306 --> 11:45.766 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

11:45.996 --> 11:48.796 align:middle
Should we be viewing today's
cut as the Committee taking

11:48.796 --> 11:51.236 align:middle
out some insurance
against the possibility

11:51.496 --> 11:53.486 align:middle
that the labor market
is at risk of weakening,

11:53.776 --> 11:55.516 align:middle
or is it the Committee's
view that the dynamics

11:55.516 --> 11:58.146 align:middle
of a downturn are
already in place?

11:58.146 --> 12:00.426 align:middle
I guess I'm just, you know,
trying to square the shift

12:00.426 --> 12:03.726 align:middle
in the rate forecast in
the SEP towards more cuts

12:03.826 --> 12:07.196 align:middle
than just three months ago
with the fact that the forecast

12:07.196 --> 12:08.586 align:middle
for unemployment didn't change.

12:08.586 --> 12:09.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:09.446 --> 12:11.376 align:middle
Yeah, I think you could
think of this, in a way,

12:11.376 --> 12:14.996 align:middle
as a risk-management cut,
because if you look at the SEP,

12:14.996 --> 12:19.286 align:middle
actually the projections
for growth this year

12:19.286 --> 12:22.226 align:middle
and next actually ticked up
just a little bit and inflation

12:22.226 --> 12:23.456 align:middle
and unemployment
didn't really move.

12:23.456 --> 12:24.736 align:middle
So what's different now?

12:24.736 --> 12:27.566 align:middle
What's different now is that
you see a very different picture

12:27.566 --> 12:29.056 align:middle
of the risks to the
labor market.

12:29.506 --> 12:33.346 align:middle
You've seen-you know, we were
looking at 150,000 jobs a month

12:33.346 --> 12:36.046 align:middle
at the time of the last meeting,
and now we see the revisions

12:36.046 --> 12:37.226 align:middle
and we see the new numbers.

12:37.686 --> 12:39.806 align:middle
And I didn't-I don't want
to put too much emphasis

12:39.806 --> 12:43.006 align:middle
on payroll job creation, but
it's just one of the things

12:43.006 --> 12:46.036 align:middle
that suggests that the labor
market is really cooling off.

12:46.036 --> 12:49.956 align:middle
And that tells you that it's
time to take that into account

12:49.956 --> 12:51.236 align:middle
in our, you know, in our policy.

12:51.946 --> 12:52.376 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

12:52.376 --> 12:54.166 align:middle
So just looking at
the SEP once again,

12:54.166 --> 12:56.076 align:middle
the median participant
has inflation higher

12:56.076 --> 12:58.766 align:middle
than previously expected
by the end of next year

12:59.056 --> 13:02.446 align:middle
and the Fed not getting back to
the 2 percent target until 2028.

13:02.446 --> 13:04.776 align:middle
So I guess I'm just wondering
how you characterize the risk

13:04.776 --> 13:07.496 align:middle
to price pressures of
kicking off a series

13:07.496 --> 13:08.386 align:middle
of cuts at this point.

13:08.776 --> 13:09.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:09.376 --> 13:11.916 align:middle
Yeah, so I would
look at it this way.

13:12.666 --> 13:15.736 align:middle
We fully understand and
appreciate that we need

13:15.736 --> 13:20.726 align:middle
to remain fully committed to
restoring 2 percent inflation

13:21.116 --> 13:22.966 align:middle
on a sustained basis,
and we will do that.

13:23.626 --> 13:24.836 align:middle
At the same time, we've got

13:24.836 --> 13:26.676 align:middle
to weigh the risks
to the two goals.

13:26.676 --> 13:31.716 align:middle
And I would say since-really,
since April, to me the risks

13:31.716 --> 13:35.186 align:middle
of higher and more persistent
inflation have probably become a

13:35.186 --> 13:35.906 align:middle
little less.

13:36.066 --> 13:38.716 align:middle
And that's partly because the
labor market has softened,

13:38.826 --> 13:40.346 align:middle
GDP growth has slowed.

13:40.346 --> 13:47.906 align:middle
And so I would just say that
the risks there have been less

13:48.516 --> 13:50.426 align:middle
than one might think.

13:51.036 --> 13:55.076 align:middle
And in terms of the labor
market, what we're seeing is,

13:55.356 --> 13:57.976 align:middle
unemployment is still low-it's
still at a relatively low rate,

13:57.976 --> 13:59.706 align:middle
but we're seeing downside risks.

14:00.306 --> 14:04.326 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

14:05.076 --> 14:07.936 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

14:08.366 --> 14:12.286 align:middle
I'm a little confused
by your explanation

14:12.566 --> 14:16.166 align:middle
of the Fed cutting rates because
of unemployment if you think

14:16.166 --> 14:19.296 align:middle
that most of what's happening in
the employment area is related

14:19.296 --> 14:23.376 align:middle
to immigration, which your
rate cuts wouldn't address.

14:23.496 --> 14:28.566 align:middle
How do you see that as more
important than inflation,

14:28.566 --> 14:31.696 align:middle
which has remained almost
a full percentage point

14:31.696 --> 14:32.356 align:middle
above your target?

14:32.606 --> 14:33.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:33.026 --> 14:34.956 align:middle
Well, I was-I was saying
that what's happening

14:34.956 --> 14:37.966 align:middle
in the labor market has
more to do with immigration

14:37.966 --> 14:39.156 align:middle
than it has to do with tariffs.

14:39.156 --> 14:40.656 align:middle
That was the question
I was answering.

14:41.196 --> 14:43.326 align:middle
So I wouldn't say that
all of what's happening

14:43.326 --> 14:45.146 align:middle
in the labor market
is due to tariffs.

14:45.146 --> 14:48.466 align:middle
I mean, you clearly have a
slowing due to immigration.

14:48.466 --> 14:50.066 align:middle
You clearly have a
slowing in demand,

14:50.476 --> 14:53.366 align:middle
which is now perhaps
more than that in supply.

14:53.366 --> 14:56.096 align:middle
And we know that because the
unemployment rate has ticked up.

14:56.856 --> 14:59.436 align:middle
So that's how-that's
what I meant by that.

14:59.436 --> 15:00.126 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

15:00.126 --> 15:05.576 align:middle
If I could follow up: Every year
since 2015, the SEP has forecast

15:05.576 --> 15:07.666 align:middle
that you would hit your
target two years later.

15:08.386 --> 15:11.276 align:middle
And this year, this
SEP says you're going

15:11.276 --> 15:13.236 align:middle
to hit your target
two years later.

15:13.646 --> 15:16.316 align:middle
Two percent does not
seem to be in sight.

15:16.366 --> 15:20.056 align:middle
Does that suggest that the
2 percent target is not

15:20.056 --> 15:21.146 align:middle
really achievable?

15:21.546 --> 15:24.666 align:middle
And does this present any
credibility problems for you

15:25.086 --> 15:28.156 align:middle
in telling people that that's
what you're going to do,

15:28.156 --> 15:29.376 align:middle
if you can never reach it?

15:29.376 --> 15:30.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:30.056 --> 15:30.926 align:middle
Well, I mean, you're right.

15:30.926 --> 15:33.126 align:middle
It does say we're going to
get to 2 percent inflation

15:33.126 --> 15:34.876 align:middle
in 2028-at the end of '28.

15:34.926 --> 15:36.016 align:middle
But that's-you know,

15:36.016 --> 15:38.156 align:middle
that's literally how you put
the projections together.

15:38.206 --> 15:42.496 align:middle
You're writing down a rate path
which is designed to create,

15:42.876 --> 15:46.036 align:middle
over the course of the
SEP, you know, time frame,

15:46.576 --> 15:48.736 align:middle
2 percent inflation, and
maximum employment too.

15:48.736 --> 15:50.526 align:middle
So that's, that's all that is.

15:50.976 --> 15:54.306 align:middle
You know, we don't-no one really
knows where the economy will be

15:54.306 --> 15:58.596 align:middle
in three years, but the nature
of the exercise is to write

15:58.596 --> 16:00.826 align:middle
down policy that you
believe would return

16:00.826 --> 16:03.606 align:middle
to the 2 percent goal
over the-at least

16:03.606 --> 16:05.396 align:middle
by the end of the exercise.

16:07.776 --> 16:08.796 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

16:09.666 --> 16:10.496 align:middle
Thanks so much.

16:10.496 --> 16:12.756 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

16:13.166 --> 16:14.566 align:middle
The latest inflation
report shows

16:14.566 --> 16:17.316 align:middle
that prices are still going
up across key categories

16:17.316 --> 16:19.226 align:middle
for many households,
including groceries.

16:19.226 --> 16:22.156 align:middle
What will the Fed do
if prices pick up more?

16:22.756 --> 16:23.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:23.996 --> 16:27.316 align:middle
So our expectation, and you
can see this consistently

16:27.316 --> 16:31.686 align:middle
through the year, has been
that inflation will move

16:31.686 --> 16:34.086 align:middle
up this year, but
that the-basically,

16:34.086 --> 16:37.566 align:middle
because of the effects on
goods prices from tariffs-but

16:37.606 --> 16:41.956 align:middle
that those will turn out to
be a one-time price increase,

16:41.956 --> 16:44.096 align:middle
as opposed to creating
an inflationary process.

16:44.256 --> 16:45.546 align:middle
That's been our forecast.

16:46.006 --> 16:48.916 align:middle
Pretty much every-all the
individual forecasts say that.

16:50.226 --> 16:52.206 align:middle
We can't just assume
that, though, right?

16:52.206 --> 16:54.906 align:middle
We have to-our job is
literally to make sure

16:54.906 --> 16:56.076 align:middle
that that is what happens.

16:56.076 --> 16:57.406 align:middle
And we will do that job.

16:57.996 --> 17:00.346 align:middle
Right now, the situation
we're in is

17:00.346 --> 17:04.056 align:middle
that we see inflation-we
continue

17:04.056 --> 17:05.056 align:middle
to expect it to move up.

17:05.056 --> 17:06.886 align:middle
Maybe not as high as we
would have expected it

17:06.886 --> 17:09.646 align:middle
to move up a few months ago.

17:09.646 --> 17:12.786 align:middle
The pass-through
into-of the tariffs

17:12.786 --> 17:14.926 align:middle
into inflation has been
slower and smaller.

17:15.226 --> 17:16.586 align:middle
The labor market has softened.

17:16.876 --> 17:21.026 align:middle
So the case for there being a
persistent inflation outbreak

17:21.326 --> 17:22.256 align:middle
is less.

17:22.256 --> 17:25.836 align:middle
And so that's why we think
it's time for us really

17:25.836 --> 17:29.136 align:middle
to acknowledge that the risks
to the other mandate have grown,

17:29.136 --> 17:32.696 align:middle
and that we should move in
the direction of neutral.

17:32.696 --> 17:33.456 align:middle
So what will we do?

17:33.456 --> 17:34.766 align:middle
We'll do what we need to do.

17:35.046 --> 17:37.996 align:middle
But we have two mandates,
and we try to balance them.

17:37.996 --> 17:42.276 align:middle
For a long time, our framework
says that when our two goals are

17:42.276 --> 17:44.556 align:middle
in tension-this is quite
an unusual situation.

17:45.026 --> 17:46.186 align:middle
How do we decide what to do?

17:46.186 --> 17:48.396 align:middle
Because our tools can't
do two things at once.

17:49.046 --> 17:52.796 align:middle
What we do is we ask, which is
farther from-how far is each

17:52.796 --> 17:55.306 align:middle
from the goal, and how long is
it expected to get to the goal?

17:55.826 --> 17:57.896 align:middle
So and then that's-we
think about those things,

17:57.896 --> 17:59.636 align:middle
and we see-as I mentioned,

17:59.636 --> 18:03.206 align:middle
we have been-our policy had been
really skewed toward inflation

18:03.206 --> 18:04.236 align:middle
for a long time, really.

18:04.686 --> 18:07.596 align:middle
Now we see that there's
downside risk clearly

18:07.596 --> 18:08.316 align:middle
in the labor market.

18:08.316 --> 18:09.666 align:middle
And so we're moving
in a direction

18:09.666 --> 18:10.876 align:middle
of more neutral policy.

18:10.876 --> 18:11.586 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

18:11.586 --> 18:14.106 align:middle
How concerned are you about
the slowdown in the jobs market

18:14.106 --> 18:15.166 align:middle
for households at home,

18:15.166 --> 18:17.436 align:middle
especially younger Americans
struggling to land a job?

18:17.496 --> 18:17.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:18.226 --> 18:20.296 align:middle
So it's an interesting
labor market.

18:20.296 --> 18:23.196 align:middle
And obviously we
think it's appropriate

18:23.306 --> 18:26.536 align:middle
that we reduce our rates so
that we become more neutral,

18:26.536 --> 18:28.096 align:middle
which will be-which, presumably,

18:28.096 --> 18:29.586 align:middle
will be better for
the labor market.

18:30.466 --> 18:33.856 align:middle
You see people who
are sort of more

18:33.856 --> 18:36.636 align:middle
at the margins-so kids
coming out of college

18:36.706 --> 18:38.066 align:middle
and younger people,

18:38.746 --> 18:43.236 align:middle
minorities-are having a
hard time finding jobs.

18:43.236 --> 18:46.446 align:middle
The overall job-finding
rate is very, very low.

18:46.956 --> 18:49.566 align:middle
However, the layoff
rate is also very low.

18:49.566 --> 18:51.606 align:middle
So you've got a low-a
low-firing,

18:51.606 --> 18:53.276 align:middle
low-hiring environment.

18:53.276 --> 18:56.276 align:middle
And the concern is that if
you start to see layoffs,

18:56.826 --> 18:58.796 align:middle
the people who are laid off
won't-there won't be a lot

18:58.796 --> 18:59.676 align:middle
of hiring going on.

18:59.676 --> 19:03.746 align:middle
So that could very quickly
flow into higher unemployment.

19:04.156 --> 19:08.116 align:middle
In a healthier economy,
healthier labor market,

19:08.116 --> 19:09.256 align:middle
there would be jobs
for those people.

19:09.256 --> 19:11.246 align:middle
But now the hiring
rate is very, very low.

19:11.246 --> 19:14.696 align:middle
So that's been a growing concern
over the last few months,

19:15.246 --> 19:17.296 align:middle
and it's one of the reasons
why we think it's appropriate

19:17.296 --> 19:21.286 align:middle
that we begin to shift our
policy focus toward a more

19:21.286 --> 19:21.886 align:middle
balanced one.

19:23.536 --> 19:26.686 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

19:27.426 --> 19:28.736 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

19:28.736 --> 19:32.976 align:middle
Mr. Chair, in the past during
rate cuts you used the word

19:33.076 --> 19:33.926 align:middle
"recalibration."

19:33.926 --> 19:37.956 align:middle
And I wonder if you pointedly
did not use it this time.

19:37.956 --> 19:40.426 align:middle
And, in fact, when you
said "policy is not

19:40.426 --> 19:42.646 align:middle
on a preset course," did
you mean that as sort

19:42.646 --> 19:45.526 align:middle
of the opposite of
recalibration?

19:45.526 --> 19:48.956 align:middle
Are we meeting to meeting,
data point by data point?

19:48.956 --> 19:51.906 align:middle
Are we in the process here
of getting back to neutral?

19:52.186 --> 19:52.506 align:middle
Thank you.

19:52.546 --> 19:53.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:54.106 --> 19:57.216 align:middle
So I think we are-we're in a
meeting-by-meeting situation,

19:57.216 --> 19:59.146 align:middle
and we're going to be
looking at the data.

19:59.146 --> 20:01.916 align:middle
You know, let me say a word
about the SEP, really, here.

20:02.376 --> 20:04.186 align:middle
So we often point this out.

20:04.186 --> 20:09.106 align:middle
What the SEP is, it's an
accumulation-a cumulation

20:09.646 --> 20:11.096 align:middle
of the individual projections

20:11.096 --> 20:14.776 align:middle
of 19 people showing what they
believe at a particular moment

20:14.776 --> 20:18.016 align:middle
in time to be the most likely
path for the economy and, thus,

20:18.156 --> 20:20.356 align:middle
the appropriate path for
monetary policy as well.

20:20.966 --> 20:24.596 align:middle
And as you know, we don't-we
don't debate or try to agree

20:24.596 --> 20:26.816 align:middle
on what that is; we just write
them down and cumulate them.

20:26.816 --> 20:28.056 align:middle
We do sometimes discuss them.

20:28.526 --> 20:30.396 align:middle
And so we always say we're
not on a preset path,

20:30.396 --> 20:32.396 align:middle
and we really mean that.

20:32.396 --> 20:34.826 align:middle
The actual decisions we
make are going to be based

20:34.826 --> 20:37.066 align:middle
on the incoming data,
the evolving outlook,

20:37.066 --> 20:38.306 align:middle
and the balance of risks

20:38.626 --> 20:40.446 align:middle
at the time the decisions
are actually made.

20:40.586 --> 20:45.766 align:middle
So you will have seen that we
have 10 participants out of 19

20:46.186 --> 20:49.156 align:middle
who wrote down two or more cuts
for the remainder of the year,

20:49.586 --> 20:53.436 align:middle
and 9 who wrote down fewer
than-in fact, in a good number

20:53.436 --> 20:54.816 align:middle
of cases no more cuts.

20:54.846 --> 20:58.796 align:middle
So rather than looking at-so
rather than looking at this

20:58.796 --> 21:01.456 align:middle
as certainty, I would
encourage people, as always,

21:01.786 --> 21:05.256 align:middle
to look at the SEP as-through
the lens of probability.

21:05.616 --> 21:07.926 align:middle
And so there are-there are
different possible outcomes

21:08.396 --> 21:11.476 align:middle
and likelihoods rather
than "this one is certain,

21:11.476 --> 21:12.606 align:middle
and this one isn't happening."

21:12.646 --> 21:14.006 align:middle
So that's what I would say.

21:14.336 --> 21:15.476 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

21:15.476 --> 21:17.796 align:middle
Thank you for getting to my
follow-up question, which was,

21:17.796 --> 21:22.916 align:middle
is the dispersion in the outlook
a sign of uncertainty of policy

21:22.916 --> 21:24.796 align:middle
in the-in the meetings to come?

21:24.796 --> 21:25.186 align:middle
Thank you.

21:25.366 --> 21:25.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:25.806 --> 21:29.136 align:middle
You know, it is such
an unusual situation.

21:29.976 --> 21:32.756 align:middle
Ordinarily, when the
labor market is weak,

21:32.756 --> 21:36.396 align:middle
inflation is low; and when the
labor market is really strong,

21:36.396 --> 21:38.306 align:middle
that's when you've got to
be careful about inflation.

21:38.306 --> 21:41.166 align:middle
So we have a situation where
we have two-sided risk,

21:41.896 --> 21:44.316 align:middle
and that means there's
no risk-free path.

21:44.316 --> 21:47.926 align:middle
And so it's quite a difficult
situation for policymakers.

21:47.926 --> 21:51.076 align:middle
And it's not at all
surprising to me

21:51.076 --> 21:53.406 align:middle
that you have a range of views.

21:53.406 --> 21:57.376 align:middle
It's not so much about having
different views for the path

21:57.376 --> 21:59.946 align:middle
of the economy, but-it's partly
that, but it's also partly

21:59.946 --> 22:02.376 align:middle
about what's the right
thing to do in light

22:02.376 --> 22:05.126 align:middle
of this-of the tension
between the two goals.

22:05.126 --> 22:06.116 align:middle
How do you weight them?

22:06.566 --> 22:08.636 align:middle
How worried are you about
one versus the other?

22:08.636 --> 22:10.576 align:middle
And so it's natural.

22:10.576 --> 22:12.256 align:middle
I think it would
actually be surprising

22:12.516 --> 22:16.116 align:middle
if you didn't have a pretty wide
range of views based, you know,

22:16.116 --> 22:20.846 align:middle
in this kind of highly
unusual situation, and we do.

22:20.906 --> 22:22.696 align:middle
But, you know, we get together,

22:22.696 --> 22:25.336 align:middle
we discuss-we have
a great discussion,

22:25.336 --> 22:27.376 align:middle
and then we decide
what to do and we act.

22:27.426 --> 22:31.666 align:middle
But you're right that it is-it's
a wide dispersion of views,

22:31.666 --> 22:33.736 align:middle
and I think that's
understandable and natural

22:33.736 --> 22:34.686 align:middle
in the current situation.

22:35.986 --> 22:39.066 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

22:39.856 --> 22:42.246 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

22:42.346 --> 22:45.206 align:middle
You've talked a lot about Fed
independence and the importance

22:45.206 --> 22:49.846 align:middle
of it over the years, but
as markets have questions

22:49.846 --> 22:52.596 align:middle
about what exactly President
Trump's intentions are

22:52.596 --> 22:54.606 align:middle
with the Fed, what
would you point

22:54.606 --> 22:58.186 align:middle
to as the things they should
be watching to determine

22:58.256 --> 23:02.176 align:middle
that the Fed is still making
decisions based on, you know,

23:02.176 --> 23:04.666 align:middle
the economic outlook rather
than political considerations?

23:05.386 --> 23:06.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:06.116 --> 23:09.846 align:middle
Look, it's deeply in our culture
to do-to do our work based

23:09.846 --> 23:13.836 align:middle
on the incoming data and
never consider anything else.

23:13.836 --> 23:15.446 align:middle
That's just-everybody here who's

23:15.446 --> 23:17.656 align:middle
at the Fed really feels
strongly about that way.

23:17.656 --> 23:21.236 align:middle
So, you know, you'll know
it by the way we talk

23:21.236 --> 23:23.956 align:middle
about what we're doing, by
the speeches that people give,

23:23.956 --> 23:25.236 align:middle
by the decisions that we make.

23:25.236 --> 23:27.066 align:middle
You'll know that we're just
still going to do that.

23:27.066 --> 23:27.826 align:middle
That's all we do.

23:28.446 --> 23:31.416 align:middle
And we don't-we don't frame
these questions at all

23:31.416 --> 23:34.536 align:middle
or see them in terms
of political outcomes.

23:34.536 --> 23:37.186 align:middle
I think when you get to
another part of Washington,

23:37.486 --> 23:39.886 align:middle
everything is seen through
the lens of, does it help

23:39.886 --> 23:43.006 align:middle
or hurt this political
party, this politician?

23:43.006 --> 23:44.306 align:middle
You know, that's the framework.

23:44.306 --> 23:45.996 align:middle
And I think people
find it hard to believe

23:45.996 --> 23:48.586 align:middle
that that's just-that is
not at all the way we think

23:48.586 --> 23:49.956 align:middle
about things at the Fed.

23:49.956 --> 23:51.326 align:middle
We're taking a longer
perspective.

23:51.926 --> 23:53.126 align:middle
We're trying to, you know,

23:53.126 --> 23:54.916 align:middle
serve the American
people as best we can.

23:55.466 --> 23:58.296 align:middle
So I think-I think you
would be able to tell.

23:58.296 --> 24:00.506 align:middle
I don't-I don't believe
we'll ever get to that place.

24:01.046 --> 24:03.986 align:middle
You know, I would say we're
doing our work exactly

24:03.986 --> 24:05.006 align:middle
as we always have now,

24:05.516 --> 24:07.936 align:middle
and people are-they're
making their arguments,

24:07.936 --> 24:10.186 align:middle
and we're having,
really, a great discussion

24:10.186 --> 24:11.566 align:middle
around these challenging issues.

24:12.296 --> 24:13.846 align:middle
So- VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:13.846 --> 24:17.306 align:middle
Do you see the court case around
Lisa Cook as being related

24:17.306 --> 24:18.606 align:middle
to questions of Fed
independence?

24:19.206 --> 24:19.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:19.636 --> 24:21.486 align:middle
You know, I see it
as a court case

24:21.486 --> 24:24.996 align:middle
that it would be inappropriate
for me to comment on.

24:25.676 --> 24:27.116 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:27.856 --> 24:30.266 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

24:30.266 --> 24:32.116 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

24:32.116 --> 24:34.996 align:middle
So we saw the preliminary
benchmark revisions,

24:34.996 --> 24:37.056 align:middle
down 911,000.

24:37.436 --> 24:40.746 align:middle
The revisions for June were the
first negative revisions we've

24:40.746 --> 24:42.486 align:middle
seen since December of 2020.

24:42.686 --> 24:45.636 align:middle
You know, how can the Fed base
important decisions on rate

24:45.956 --> 24:48.186 align:middle
and what to do with the
interest-with the interest rates

24:48.356 --> 24:49.866 align:middle
on data that seems to be,

24:49.866 --> 24:51.166 align:middle
as you've called it
in the past, noisy?

24:51.236 --> 24:52.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:52.246 --> 24:56.126 align:middle
So, on the QCEW, the revision

24:56.126 --> 24:58.366 align:middle
that we saw was almost
exactly what we expected.

24:58.366 --> 25:01.316 align:middle
It was amazing how close
the expectation was.

25:01.316 --> 25:05.796 align:middle
And the reason for that is for
the last bunch of quarters,

25:06.426 --> 25:09.966 align:middle
there's been a-almost a
predictable overcount.

25:10.606 --> 25:11.856 align:middle
And I think the, you know,

25:11.856 --> 25:14.706 align:middle
the Bureau of Labor Statistics
really does understand this,

25:14.706 --> 25:15.936 align:middle
and they're working
hard to fix it.

25:15.936 --> 25:18.386 align:middle
It's got to do-of course,
it has something to do

25:18.386 --> 25:19.656 align:middle
with low response rates.

25:19.656 --> 25:20.506 align:middle
But it's also got to do

25:20.506 --> 25:22.166 align:middle
with what's called
the birth-death model,

25:22.826 --> 25:25.146 align:middle
because a good amount
of job creation happens

25:25.696 --> 25:28.076 align:middle
around new companies,
and how many go

25:28.076 --> 25:29.356 align:middle
out of business, how
many are founded.

25:29.356 --> 25:30.906 align:middle
And it's just really hard.

25:30.906 --> 25:31.956 align:middle
You can't survey for that.

25:31.956 --> 25:33.516 align:middle
You've got to have a
model that predicts that.

25:33.946 --> 25:35.046 align:middle
And it's quite difficult to do,

25:35.046 --> 25:37.736 align:middle
especially when the economy
is undergoing big changes.

25:37.736 --> 25:41.116 align:middle
And they've been working on that
and are making progress on it.

25:41.556 --> 25:45.746 align:middle
But, you know, the data we
get is still well good enough

25:45.746 --> 25:46.696 align:middle
for us to do our work.

25:46.696 --> 25:50.526 align:middle
To the extent we have issues
around data right now,

25:50.526 --> 25:52.136 align:middle
it's about low response rates.

25:52.556 --> 25:56.156 align:middle
That's happened all
over-all over, really.

25:56.206 --> 25:58.686 align:middle
Response rates are just
lower to surveys now, both in

25:58.686 --> 25:59.526 align:middle
and out of the government.

26:00.146 --> 26:01.576 align:middle
And it's no great secret.

26:01.576 --> 26:03.526 align:middle
You know, we want
higher response rates,

26:03.526 --> 26:06.166 align:middle
and we need those to have,
you know, less volatile data.

26:06.536 --> 26:09.806 align:middle
And the way to get that is to
make sure that the agencies

26:09.806 --> 26:12.046 align:middle
that collect the data
have sufficient resources

26:12.086 --> 26:14.486 align:middle
to drive higher response rates.

26:14.706 --> 26:16.136 align:middle
It's not a complicated problem.

26:16.136 --> 26:17.166 align:middle
But that's what it takes.

26:17.166 --> 26:18.006 align:middle
It's not a mystery.

26:18.416 --> 26:19.066 align:middle
That's what it takes.

26:19.066 --> 26:22.666 align:middle
The other thing I'll say,
Edward, is in the case

26:22.666 --> 26:24.236 align:middle
of the job creation,

26:24.626 --> 26:27.606 align:middle
the first-the response rate is
quite low for the first month,

26:27.606 --> 26:28.866 align:middle
or lower for the first month.

26:28.866 --> 26:31.036 align:middle
By the time you get to
the second or third month,

26:31.136 --> 26:33.316 align:middle
you're still collecting
responses

26:33.316 --> 26:35.316 align:middle
for that last month-for
that prior month.

26:35.576 --> 26:38.756 align:middle
And you get to the place where
the data are much more reliable

26:38.756 --> 26:40.496 align:middle
by the second, and
certainly the third, month.

26:40.876 --> 26:42.806 align:middle
So it's not that we don't
get the data-it's just

26:42.806 --> 26:45.376 align:middle
that we get it a little later.

26:45.376 --> 26:46.206 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

26:46.396 --> 26:48.896 align:middle
But if-well, for the benchmarks,
if that holds, it's 51 percent

26:48.896 --> 26:51.256 align:middle
of the jobs that we thought
were there weren't really there.

26:51.646 --> 26:54.616 align:middle
It shows a weaker market-job
market coming into this year.

26:54.976 --> 26:56.706 align:middle
If you would have
had that information,

26:56.706 --> 26:58.596 align:middle
would it have changed
your mind related

26:58.596 --> 27:00.466 align:middle
to where the interest
rates should be?

27:00.466 --> 27:01.456 align:middle
Should there have
been a cut earlier?

27:01.756 --> 27:02.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:02.076 --> 27:03.346 align:middle
You know, we have
to live life looking

27:03.396 --> 27:04.336 align:middle
through the windshield rather

27:04.336 --> 27:06.026 align:middle
than the rearview
mirror, as you know.

27:06.136 --> 27:09.716 align:middle
And all I can tell you is
we see where we are now,

27:09.716 --> 27:10.936 align:middle
and we take appropriate action.

27:10.936 --> 27:12.406 align:middle
And we took that
appropriate action today.

27:13.936 --> 27:16.556 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:16.966 --> 27:18.906 align:middle
Thanks. Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

27:19.146 --> 27:22.256 align:middle
So, as you mentioned a
minute ago, some margins

27:22.256 --> 27:23.716 align:middle
of the job market would suggest

27:23.716 --> 27:25.246 align:middle
that the slide's
already happening.

27:25.696 --> 27:28.706 align:middle
The Black unemployment rate
in August was above 7 percent,

27:28.706 --> 27:32.606 align:middle
declining workweek, difficulty
among college graduates finding

27:32.606 --> 27:34.946 align:middle
work, high-rising
youth unemployment.

27:35.426 --> 27:38.596 align:middle
Why do you think a ¼
percentage point now is going

27:38.596 --> 27:39.406 align:middle
to arrest that?

27:40.476 --> 27:41.346 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:41.736 --> 27:42.896 align:middle
Well, I hadn't say

27:42.896 --> 27:44.796 align:middle
that I thought a ¼ point
would make a huge difference

27:44.826 --> 27:45.316 align:middle
to the economy.

27:45.316 --> 27:47.996 align:middle
But you've got to look at the
whole path of rates, right?

27:48.196 --> 27:50.936 align:middle
And the market has
already-has already been baking

27:50.936 --> 27:52.966 align:middle
in expectations.

27:52.966 --> 27:55.586 align:middle
I mean, our market works
through expectations, right?

27:55.586 --> 27:57.916 align:middle
So I think our policy
path really does matter.

27:58.486 --> 28:00.516 align:middle
And I think it's important
that we use our tools

28:00.516 --> 28:03.306 align:middle
to support the labor market
when we do see signs like that.

28:03.306 --> 28:04.186 align:middle
I did mention that, you know,

28:04.186 --> 28:07.986 align:middle
you see minority
unemployment going up,

28:07.986 --> 28:09.606 align:middle
you see younger people-people

28:09.606 --> 28:11.576 align:middle
who are more vulnerable
economically,

28:11.866 --> 28:13.596 align:middle
more susceptible
to economic cycles.

28:13.976 --> 28:15.086 align:middle
That's one of the reasons,

28:15.086 --> 28:19.246 align:middle
in addition to just overall
lower payroll job creation,

28:19.246 --> 28:22.156 align:middle
that shows you that at the
margin the labor market

28:22.156 --> 28:22.626 align:middle
is weakening.

28:22.626 --> 28:24.896 align:middle
I would also point to
labor force participation.

28:25.176 --> 28:27.206 align:middle
Some part of the
significant decline

28:27.206 --> 28:28.536 align:middle
in labor force participation

28:28.536 --> 28:32.936 align:middle
over the last year has probably
been cyclical in nature,

28:32.936 --> 28:34.686 align:middle
rather than just the
usual aging process.

28:34.686 --> 28:35.966 align:middle
So we put all those together,

28:36.476 --> 28:39.166 align:middle
and we see that the labor
market is softening,

28:39.166 --> 28:41.326 align:middle
and we don't need it to soften
any more, don't want it to.

28:41.806 --> 28:43.366 align:middle
So we use our tools.

28:43.466 --> 28:48.036 align:middle
And, you know, it starts with
a 25 basis point rate cut,

28:48.316 --> 28:51.136 align:middle
but we-you know, the market's
also pricing in a rate path.

28:51.136 --> 28:53.196 align:middle
I'm not blessing what the
market's doing at all.

28:53.196 --> 28:55.546 align:middle
I'm just saying it's
not just one action.

28:56.506 --> 28:57.306 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

28:57.456 --> 28:59.206 align:middle
As a follow-up to that:

28:59.206 --> 29:02.076 align:middle
The growth mix right now
seems very concentrated

29:02.076 --> 29:06.176 align:middle
in investment and, on the
consumer spending side,

29:06.556 --> 29:07.966 align:middle
in higher-income groups.

29:08.416 --> 29:11.696 align:middle
Do you feel that that's
an unsustainable mix

29:11.696 --> 29:13.676 align:middle
for the economy, moving forward?

29:14.166 --> 29:15.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:15.676 --> 29:16.346 align:middle
I wouldn't say that.

29:16.346 --> 29:17.266 align:middle
I mean, you're right.

29:17.266 --> 29:21.826 align:middle
Those are two-we're getting
unusually large amounts

29:21.826 --> 29:24.756 align:middle
of economic activity
through the AI build-out

29:24.756 --> 29:25.946 align:middle
and corporate investment.

29:26.746 --> 29:29.826 align:middle
I don't know how
long that will go on.

29:29.826 --> 29:31.156 align:middle
No one does.

29:31.156 --> 29:34.296 align:middle
In terms of spending, you saw
the consumer spending numbers

29:34.296 --> 29:36.886 align:middle
were well above expectations,

29:36.886 --> 29:41.586 align:middle
and that may well be skewed
toward higher-earning consumers.

29:41.586 --> 29:43.856 align:middle
There's a lot of anecdotal
evidence to suggest that.

29:44.106 --> 29:45.846 align:middle
Nonetheless, it's spending.

29:46.316 --> 29:51.996 align:middle
So, I mean, I think the economy
is-you know, it's moving along.

29:51.996 --> 29:56.056 align:middle
Economic growth is going to be
1½ percent or better this year,

29:56.056 --> 29:56.836 align:middle
maybe a little better.

29:57.496 --> 29:59.436 align:middle
Forecasts have been
coming up, as you can see.

29:59.576 --> 30:06.556 align:middle
So labor market is-unemployment
is low, but-you know,

30:06.556 --> 30:09.436 align:middle
downside risks-but it's still
a low unemployment rate.

30:09.606 --> 30:12.506 align:middle
So that's how we see it.

30:13.516 --> 30:16.316 align:middle
STEPHANIE RUHLE.

30:16.966 --> 30:19.026 align:middle
Hi. Stephanie Ruhle, MSNBC.

30:19.396 --> 30:21.816 align:middle
Treasury Secretary
Scott Bessent has said

30:21.816 --> 30:25.026 align:middle
that the Federal Reserve
suffers from "mission creep"

30:25.026 --> 30:26.386 align:middle
and institutional bloat.

30:27.096 --> 30:29.076 align:middle
He's now calling for
an independent review.

30:29.456 --> 30:32.506 align:middle
Would you support an independent
review, or are you open

30:32.506 --> 30:34.776 align:middle
to any sort of reform
in any areas of the Fed?

30:35.446 --> 30:36.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:36.726 --> 30:38.536 align:middle
I-of course, I'm
not going to comment

30:38.536 --> 30:40.296 align:middle
on anything the secretary
says or, really,

30:40.296 --> 30:42.906 align:middle
any other officer says.

30:43.416 --> 30:46.926 align:middle
So in terms of reform
at the Fed, you know,

30:47.206 --> 30:50.276 align:middle
we just-we just went
through a lengthy

30:50.276 --> 30:52.276 align:middle
and I think very
successful process

30:52.276 --> 30:55.406 align:middle
of updating our monetary
policy framework.

30:56.206 --> 31:00.236 align:middle
I would say there's a lot of
work going on behind the scenes

31:00.326 --> 31:03.426 align:middle
around the assets we have

31:03.426 --> 31:06.826 align:middle
in the Federal Reserve
System and at the Board.

31:07.396 --> 31:08.766 align:middle
Are they the right size?

31:08.766 --> 31:13.776 align:middle
We're actually going through a
10 percent head count reduction

31:14.776 --> 31:16.436 align:middle
through the whole Fed,
including the Board

31:16.436 --> 31:17.756 align:middle
and all the Reserve Banks.

31:18.456 --> 31:23.046 align:middle
Employment at the Fed at the
end of that will be basically

31:23.046 --> 31:25.306 align:middle
at the same level it was
more than 10 years ago.

31:25.306 --> 31:28.736 align:middle
So we will have had zero job
growth for more than a decade

31:29.396 --> 31:30.246 align:middle
when we're finished with that,

31:30.246 --> 31:32.386 align:middle
and I think we'll
probably do more than that.

31:32.386 --> 31:36.686 align:middle
So I think we're certainly
open to constructive criticism

31:36.686 --> 31:38.496 align:middle
and ways to do our jobs better.

31:38.496 --> 31:39.166 align:middle
STEPHANIE RUHLE.

31:39.166 --> 31:40.216 align:middle
But not an independent review?

31:40.216 --> 31:40.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:41.176 --> 31:42.736 align:middle
We're certainly open
to, you know,

31:42.736 --> 31:45.126 align:middle
to always trying to do better.

31:46.556 --> 31:47.426 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

31:49.316 --> 31:51.196 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Neil
Irwin with Axios.

31:51.536 --> 31:52.876 align:middle
There's been some debate lately

31:52.876 --> 31:54.786 align:middle
on whether AI is
already starting

31:54.786 --> 31:57.176 align:middle
to affect the labor market in
terms of lower labor demand,

31:57.536 --> 31:59.766 align:middle
higher productivity by contrast.

31:59.976 --> 32:01.756 align:middle
Do you buy that?

32:01.756 --> 32:04.016 align:middle
And if that is true, does
that have implications

32:04.016 --> 32:05.286 align:middle
for the monetary policy setting?

32:05.586 --> 32:06.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:06.066 --> 32:07.736 align:middle
So there's great
uncertainty around that.

32:07.736 --> 32:11.926 align:middle
I think my view-which is
also a bit of a guess,

32:12.066 --> 32:13.246 align:middle
but widely shared I think-is

32:13.246 --> 32:14.506 align:middle
that you are seeing
some effects,

32:14.506 --> 32:17.026 align:middle
but it's not the main
thing driving it.

32:17.236 --> 32:20.166 align:middle
And so a particular focus
on young people coming

32:20.166 --> 32:24.316 align:middle
out of college and, yeah,
there may be something there.

32:24.316 --> 32:27.486 align:middle
It may be that companies
or other institutions

32:27.486 --> 32:29.496 align:middle
that have been hiring
younger people right

32:29.496 --> 32:32.896 align:middle
out of college are
able to use AI more

32:32.896 --> 32:34.036 align:middle
than they had in the past.

32:34.036 --> 32:35.226 align:middle
That may be part of the story.

32:35.996 --> 32:38.346 align:middle
It's also part of the story,
though, that, you know,

32:38.346 --> 32:40.456 align:middle
job creation more
broadly has slowed down.

32:40.456 --> 32:42.796 align:middle
The economy has slowed down.

32:42.866 --> 32:44.876 align:middle
And so it's probably
a number of things.

32:44.876 --> 32:47.616 align:middle
But, yeah, it's probably
a factor.

32:47.616 --> 32:48.696 align:middle
Hard to say how big it is.

32:49.656 --> 32:51.286 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

32:53.906 --> 32:56.066 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

32:56.216 --> 32:59.436 align:middle
What evidence do you see of
tariffs showing up in inflation?

33:00.086 --> 33:00.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:01.226 --> 33:03.676 align:middle
Well, if you-you can
take goods-just sort

33:03.676 --> 33:05.196 align:middle
of the broad goods category,

33:05.936 --> 33:09.866 align:middle
and last year goods
inflation was negative.

33:10.056 --> 33:13.796 align:middle
If you go back 25 years, that
was the typical thing was

33:13.796 --> 33:18.036 align:middle
that goods inflation was-goods
prices generally went down,

33:18.036 --> 33:19.276 align:middle
even adjusting for quality.

33:19.846 --> 33:22.846 align:middle
So that was not the case
during the pandemic, of course.

33:22.846 --> 33:24.116 align:middle
Goods inflation went very high.

33:24.116 --> 33:26.926 align:middle
But we had essentially
returned to zero

33:26.926 --> 33:28.356 align:middle
or slightly negative inflation.

33:28.676 --> 33:31.036 align:middle
Now, I think goods
inflation over the course

33:31.036 --> 33:34.736 align:middle
of the past year is 1.2 percent,
which doesn't sound like a lot,

33:34.736 --> 33:35.676 align:middle
but it's a big change.

33:35.986 --> 33:38.396 align:middle
So we think-I mean, analysts
have different views,

33:38.396 --> 33:42.556 align:middle
but we think it's contributing,
you know, 0.3 or 0.4,

33:42.556 --> 33:43.356 align:middle
something like that,

33:43.356 --> 33:45.776 align:middle
to the current core
PCE inflation reading,

33:45.776 --> 33:46.996 align:middle
which is 2.9 percent.

33:46.996 --> 33:49.626 align:middle
So it's contributing.

33:50.056 --> 33:53.686 align:middle
What seems to be
happening is that, you know,

33:53.776 --> 33:58.716 align:middle
the tariffs are not-mostly
not being paid by exporters,

33:59.086 --> 34:04.756 align:middle
mostly being paid by,
really, the companies that sit

34:04.756 --> 34:06.656 align:middle
between the exporter
and the consumer.

34:07.036 --> 34:10.796 align:middle
So if you buy something and
you sell it through retail

34:10.796 --> 34:15.086 align:middle
or you use it to make a product,
you're probably taking a lot

34:15.086 --> 34:16.916 align:middle
of those costs on and not able

34:16.916 --> 34:18.846 align:middle
to pass it fully along
to the consumer yet.

34:18.846 --> 34:21.706 align:middle
That appears to be
what we're seeing.

34:22.476 --> 34:25.856 align:middle
All of those companies and
entities in the middle,

34:25.856 --> 34:27.926 align:middle
they'll tell you that they
have every intention of passing

34:27.926 --> 34:30.316 align:middle
that through in time, but
they're not doing that now.

34:30.356 --> 34:31.706 align:middle
To the consumer,

34:32.116 --> 34:34.536 align:middle
the pass-through has
been pretty small.

34:34.536 --> 34:36.866 align:middle
It's been slower
and later-slower

34:36.866 --> 34:39.366 align:middle
and smaller than we thought.

34:39.446 --> 34:42.876 align:middle
But the evidence is very clear
that there's some pass-through.

34:43.816 --> 34:44.666 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

34:44.666 --> 34:47.586 align:middle
I also wanted to ask
if you could share

34:47.586 --> 34:48.886 align:middle
with us the conditions

34:48.886 --> 34:52.416 align:middle
under which you might consider
leaving the Fed in May.

34:52.986 --> 34:53.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:53.506 --> 34:56.196 align:middle
I have nothing new on
that for you today.

34:58.056 --> 35:00.376 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

35:01.236 --> 35:03.666 align:middle
Hi. Catarina Saraiva
with Bloomberg News.

35:03.956 --> 35:06.286 align:middle
I just wanted to
follow up on, you know,

35:06.286 --> 35:10.576 align:middle
one of your answers just
a couple minutes ago.

35:10.686 --> 35:13.496 align:middle
You know, we've often heard
you talk about how you

35:13.496 --> 35:16.526 align:middle
and your colleagues, you know,
do not think about politics,

35:16.586 --> 35:18.166 align:middle
this does not enter the room.

35:19.496 --> 35:23.386 align:middle
But one of your new colleagues
does come from this world,

35:23.386 --> 35:25.886 align:middle
right, where everything is
seen through this framework

35:26.246 --> 35:30.186 align:middle
of politics, and of what
party is being helped.

35:30.186 --> 35:34.506 align:middle
And that person is still
employed by the White House.

35:35.166 --> 35:40.766 align:middle
How can markets and the
public interpret, you know,

35:40.986 --> 35:45.026 align:middle
some of his speeches, for
example, and then some of,

35:45.176 --> 35:46.856 align:middle
you know, the forecast
that we see today?

35:46.856 --> 35:50.186 align:middle
I mean, the median for
this year was moved

35:50.646 --> 35:53.966 align:middle
because of the introduction
of his forecast.

35:53.966 --> 35:57.046 align:middle
I'm talking about the number
of rate cuts seen this year.

35:57.776 --> 36:00.316 align:middle
What do you, you know,
what do you say to markets

36:00.316 --> 36:03.086 align:middle
and the public that are
trying to interpret, you know,

36:03.086 --> 36:04.266 align:middle
what you guys are saying?

36:04.366 --> 36:04.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:04.776 --> 36:10.056 align:middle
So there's 12 voters-19
participants, of whom 12 vote,

36:10.056 --> 36:11.636 align:middle
as you know, on a
rotating basis.

36:11.636 --> 36:16.596 align:middle
So no one voter, you know,
can really-the only way

36:16.596 --> 36:19.886 align:middle
for any voter to really
move things around is

36:19.886 --> 36:21.296 align:middle
to be incredibly persuasive.

36:21.296 --> 36:24.506 align:middle
And the only way to do that, in
the context in which we work,

36:24.506 --> 36:28.506 align:middle
is to make really strong
arguments based on the data

36:28.576 --> 36:30.896 align:middle
and one's understanding
of the economy.

36:30.896 --> 36:32.206 align:middle
That's really all that matters.

36:32.826 --> 36:34.696 align:middle
And that's how it's
going to work.

36:34.696 --> 36:40.146 align:middle
And I think that is the
way the institution-that's

36:40.146 --> 36:41.426 align:middle
in the DNA of the institution.

36:41.426 --> 36:42.236 align:middle
That's not going to change.

36:42.876 --> 36:43.376 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

36:43.376 --> 36:45.896 align:middle
And then I wanted to ask about
a Gallup poll that showed

36:46.046 --> 36:49.556 align:middle
that Americans now have more
confidence in the President

36:49.556 --> 36:51.396 align:middle
than the Federal
Reserve when it comes

36:51.396 --> 36:53.186 align:middle
to doing what's right
for the economy.

36:53.666 --> 36:54.876 align:middle
Why do you think that is?

36:54.876 --> 36:56.646 align:middle
And what's your response?

36:56.646 --> 36:57.966 align:middle
What's your message
to the public?

36:58.136 --> 36:58.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:58.506 --> 37:00.586 align:middle
Our response is we're going
to do everything we can

37:00.586 --> 37:03.016 align:middle
to use our tools to
achieve the goals

37:03.016 --> 37:04.106 align:middle
that Congress has given us.

37:04.806 --> 37:07.276 align:middle
And we're not going to get
distracted by anything.

37:07.356 --> 37:09.786 align:middle
So I think that's what
we're going to do.

37:09.786 --> 37:11.036 align:middle
We're just going to
keep doing our jobs.

37:12.486 --> 37:16.856 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

37:17.026 --> 37:18.926 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

37:19.596 --> 37:22.356 align:middle
Given the range of views
expressed prior to the meeting,

37:22.356 --> 37:25.776 align:middle
I think there was a
lot less dissent today

37:25.776 --> 37:27.486 align:middle
than a lot of people expected.

37:28.116 --> 37:31.026 align:middle
It'd be good to know just what
you think the drivers were

37:31.026 --> 37:34.326 align:middle
of coming to the very strong
consensus in the meeting.

37:34.326 --> 37:36.036 align:middle
And also, on the flip side,

37:36.036 --> 37:39.636 align:middle
to just explain why the
dot plots are really

37:39.636 --> 37:42.506 align:middle
so scattered between-you know,
someone even expecting rates

37:42.506 --> 37:45.866 align:middle
to end up higher by the end
of the year, to five cuts.

37:45.866 --> 37:48.616 align:middle
I mean, what were the kind of
range of views you had about,

37:48.926 --> 37:52.266 align:middle
on one side, why there was so
much support for a cut today,

37:52.576 --> 37:55.796 align:middle
and, on the flip side, why
there's so much divergence

37:55.796 --> 37:56.966 align:middle
about what comes next?

37:57.466 --> 37:58.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:58.186 --> 38:01.886 align:middle
So I think there's
quite a wide assessment

38:01.936 --> 38:04.996 align:middle
that the situation has
changed with respect

38:04.996 --> 38:08.966 align:middle
to the labor market, whereas we
could still say-and did say-in

38:08.966 --> 38:10.736 align:middle
July, at the time
of the July meeting,

38:10.816 --> 38:13.326 align:middle
that the labor market
was in solid condition,

38:13.326 --> 38:16.486 align:middle
and we could point to
150,000 jobs per month,

38:17.176 --> 38:18.716 align:middle
and many other things.

38:19.326 --> 38:21.596 align:middle
I think that the new data

38:21.596 --> 38:25.306 align:middle
that we've had-and it's not
just payrolls, it's other things

38:25.306 --> 38:28.576 align:middle
as well-suggests that
there really is meaningful

38:28.576 --> 38:29.456 align:middle
downside risk.

38:29.456 --> 38:31.406 align:middle
I said there was
downside risk then,

38:31.926 --> 38:35.116 align:middle
but I think that that
downside risk is now a reality.

38:35.116 --> 38:36.936 align:middle
And there's clearly
more downside risk.

38:36.936 --> 38:40.376 align:middle
So I think that was-I think
that's broadly accepted.

38:40.936 --> 38:43.646 align:middle
And so that meant different
things for different people.

38:43.916 --> 38:46.326 align:middle
You know, some wrote
down-almost everyone wrote

38:46.326 --> 38:48.076 align:middle
down support of this cut.

38:48.646 --> 38:52.076 align:middle
And some supported more
cuts, and some didn't,

38:52.426 --> 38:53.836 align:middle
as you will see from
the dot plot.

38:53.836 --> 38:55.376 align:middle
So it's just the way-that's
just the way it is.

38:55.376 --> 38:59.186 align:middle
I mean, people have-you
have people

38:59.186 --> 39:00.806 align:middle
who take this work
very seriously,

39:00.806 --> 39:02.796 align:middle
think about it all the
time, do their work,

39:03.446 --> 39:05.166 align:middle
discuss it with our colleagues.

39:05.166 --> 39:09.356 align:middle
You know, we endlessly
discuss this between ourselves.

39:09.356 --> 39:11.736 align:middle
And then we have a meeting where
we put it all out on the table.

39:12.306 --> 39:13.296 align:middle
And this is what you get.

39:13.436 --> 39:15.346 align:middle
And you're right, there
is a-there's a range

39:15.346 --> 39:17.396 align:middle
of views in the dots.

39:17.396 --> 39:19.956 align:middle
And I think that's, like I said,

39:20.936 --> 39:25.386 align:middle
very unsurprising given the
quite unusual-historically

39:25.386 --> 39:28.996 align:middle
unusual nature of the
challenges that we face.

39:28.996 --> 39:32.296 align:middle
But let's remember, though, the
unemployment rate's 4.3 percent.

39:32.666 --> 39:35.296 align:middle
The economy is growing
at 1½ percent.

39:35.756 --> 39:39.486 align:middle
So it's not a bad economy,
or anything like that.

39:39.486 --> 39:41.566 align:middle
We've seen much more
challenging economic times.

39:41.566 --> 39:44.336 align:middle
But from a policy
standpoint, the standpoint

39:44.336 --> 39:46.366 align:middle
of what we're trying
to accomplish,

39:46.366 --> 39:48.786 align:middle
it's challenging
to know what to do.

39:49.486 --> 39:51.096 align:middle
There are, as I mentioned
earlier,

39:51.096 --> 39:53.656 align:middle
there are no risk-free
paths now.

39:53.796 --> 39:56.216 align:middle
It's not incredibly
obvious what to do.

39:56.326 --> 39:59.486 align:middle
So we have to keep
our eye on inflation.

39:59.486 --> 40:02.426 align:middle
At the same time, we cannot
ignore and must keep our eye

40:03.156 --> 40:04.486 align:middle
on maximum employment,

40:04.486 --> 40:06.766 align:middle
which is-those are
our two equal goals.

40:07.326 --> 40:09.836 align:middle
And you will see that
there are just a range

40:09.836 --> 40:11.526 align:middle
of views on what to do.

40:11.526 --> 40:16.396 align:middle
Nonetheless, we came together
today at the meeting and acted

40:16.396 --> 40:18.096 align:middle
with a high degree of unity.

40:18.576 --> 40:20.766 align:middle
ARCHIE HALL.

40:22.656 --> 40:23.716 align:middle
Thank you.

40:23.716 --> 40:25.016 align:middle
Archie Hall from the Economist.

40:25.186 --> 40:28.966 align:middle
You mentioned earlier that job
creation is running below your

40:29.076 --> 40:30.556 align:middle
guess at its breakeven rate.

40:30.556 --> 40:31.916 align:middle
I'd be curious to hear
a bit more about that

40:31.916 --> 40:33.416 align:middle
and where you think
the breakeven rate is.

40:33.896 --> 40:34.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:34.726 --> 40:38.546 align:middle
You know, so there are many
different ways to calculate it

40:38.546 --> 40:41.376 align:middle
and none of them is
perfect, but, you know,

40:41.506 --> 40:43.126 align:middle
it's clearly come way down.

40:43.296 --> 40:46.656 align:middle
There are-you could say
it's somewhere between zero

40:46.656 --> 40:49.726 align:middle
and 50,000, and you'd
be right or wrong.

40:49.726 --> 40:52.856 align:middle
I mean, there's just many
different ways to do it.

40:53.266 --> 40:56.316 align:middle
So wherever it was-150,000,

40:56.316 --> 41:00.566 align:middle
200,000 a few months ago-it's
come down quite significantly.

41:00.566 --> 41:04.526 align:middle
And that's because a
very-a lower amount

41:04.526 --> 41:06.406 align:middle
of people are joining
the labor force.

41:06.766 --> 41:09.006 align:middle
The labor force is really not
growing much at this point.

41:09.006 --> 41:12.766 align:middle
And that's a lot of where the
supply of labor was coming

41:12.766 --> 41:15.006 align:middle
from over the last
three-two or three years,

41:15.206 --> 41:16.246 align:middle
so we're not getting that now.

41:16.246 --> 41:18.396 align:middle
We're also-we've gotten
much lower demand.

41:18.396 --> 41:21.606 align:middle
You know, it's interesting
that supply

41:21.606 --> 41:24.066 align:middle
and demand have really
come down together so far,

41:24.706 --> 41:27.056 align:middle
except now we do
have inflation-sorry,

41:27.056 --> 41:31.056 align:middle
unemployment ticking up
outside, just one tick outside

41:31.056 --> 41:33.216 align:middle
of the range where
it's been for a year.

41:33.806 --> 41:37.396 align:middle
Four point three percent
is still a low level, but,

41:38.036 --> 41:41.426 align:middle
you know, I think this
level of-this speedy decline

41:41.426 --> 41:44.036 align:middle
in both supply and demand
has certainly gotten

41:44.036 --> 41:44.746 align:middle
everyone's attention.

41:45.136 --> 41:45.586 align:middle
ARCHIE HALL.

41:45.586 --> 41:46.676 align:middle
A follow-up, if I may.

41:46.676 --> 41:49.256 align:middle
You mention the downside risk
to employment a fair amount,

41:49.256 --> 41:52.376 align:middle
but it's striking that measures
of kind of activity and output

41:52.376 --> 41:53.226 align:middle
for the third quarter,

41:53.226 --> 41:54.906 align:middle
those that we have,
seem pretty strong.

41:54.906 --> 41:56.916 align:middle
The Atlanta Fed's
GDPNow is very strong.

41:56.916 --> 41:59.486 align:middle
You mentioned strong
PCE numbers as well.

41:59.486 --> 42:00.856 align:middle
How do you square those things?

42:00.856 --> 42:02.426 align:middle
Is there-is there a chance
actually there could be upside

42:02.426 --> 42:03.276 align:middle
risk to the labor market

42:03.276 --> 42:04.736 align:middle
if those activity
measures are right?

42:04.736 --> 42:05.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:05.186 --> 42:06.126 align:middle
Well, that would be great.

42:06.126 --> 42:08.136 align:middle
We'd love that to happen.

42:08.676 --> 42:11.186 align:middle
So I don't know that you
see a big tension there,

42:11.186 --> 42:12.606 align:middle
but it's gratifying to see

42:12.606 --> 42:15.596 align:middle
that economic activity
is holding and-you know,

42:15.596 --> 42:18.736 align:middle
so it's a good, a good
bit from consumption.

42:18.736 --> 42:20.686 align:middle
It looks like consumption
was stronger

42:20.686 --> 42:25.136 align:middle
than expected-what we got
earlier this week, I guess.

42:25.296 --> 42:26.516 align:middle
And also we're getting,

42:26.586 --> 42:31.156 align:middle
you know-a fairly narrow
sector is producing a lot

42:31.156 --> 42:34.566 align:middle
of economic activity, which
is the AI build-out and,

42:34.836 --> 42:37.066 align:middle
you know, business investment.

42:37.126 --> 42:40.046 align:middle
So, you know, we
watch all of it.

42:40.046 --> 42:42.896 align:middle
And I would say we-you
know, we did move up,

42:43.146 --> 42:45.706 align:middle
the median for growth for
this year actually moved

42:45.706 --> 42:52.036 align:middle
up in the SEP between the June
and September SEPs, so-and,

42:52.036 --> 42:55.196 align:middle
really, the inflation and labor
market didn't change much.

42:55.196 --> 42:57.936 align:middle
It's really the risks that
we're seeing to the labor market

42:58.056 --> 43:01.696 align:middle
that were the focus
of today's decision.

43:02.286 --> 43:04.576 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

43:06.336 --> 43:07.896 align:middle
Hi. Nicole Goodkind
with Barron's.

43:07.896 --> 43:09.306 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

43:09.666 --> 43:13.016 align:middle
Given the cumulative impact
of high interest rates

43:13.016 --> 43:16.566 align:middle
on the housing sector, I'm
wondering how concerned you are

43:16.756 --> 43:18.946 align:middle
that current rate levels
are exacerbating housing

43:18.946 --> 43:20.066 align:middle
affordability issues

43:20.066 --> 43:22.056 align:middle
and potentially hindering
household formation

43:22.056 --> 43:25.996 align:middle
and wealth accumulation for
a segment of the population.

43:26.516 --> 43:27.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:27.686 --> 43:31.096 align:middle
You know, housing is an
interest-sensitive activity,

43:31.676 --> 43:34.716 align:middle
so it's at the very
center of monetary policy.

43:35.206 --> 43:39.796 align:middle
When the pandemic hit
and we cut rates to zero,

43:40.446 --> 43:44.336 align:middle
the housing companies
were incredibly grateful

43:44.336 --> 43:46.096 align:middle
and it was really-they said
that was the only thing

43:46.096 --> 43:49.136 align:middle
that kept them going was
that we cut so aggressively

43:49.136 --> 43:51.196 align:middle
and provided credit and things
like that, and they were able

43:51.196 --> 43:52.986 align:middle
to finance because we did that.

43:53.776 --> 43:55.586 align:middle
The other side of that is
when inflation gets high

43:55.586 --> 43:57.726 align:middle
and we raise rates-and
you're right,

43:57.726 --> 44:00.116 align:middle
it does burden the
housing industry.

44:00.116 --> 44:03.966 align:middle
And so rates have
come down a bit,

44:03.966 --> 44:07.376 align:middle
and as that happens we don't set
the-we don't set mortgage rates,

44:07.416 --> 44:10.986 align:middle
but our policy rate changes do
tend to affect mortgage rates.

44:11.666 --> 44:12.846 align:middle
And that has been happening.

44:12.946 --> 44:18.286 align:middle
And that'll, of course, raise
demand, lower borrowing rates

44:18.286 --> 44:21.346 align:middle
for builders, will
help, you know,

44:21.346 --> 44:23.026 align:middle
get builders-will help supply.

44:23.776 --> 44:25.726 align:middle
And so some of that
should happen.

44:25.726 --> 44:27.956 align:middle
I think most analysts
think that it would have

44:27.956 --> 44:30.446 align:middle
to be pretty big changes
for it to matter a lot

44:30.506 --> 44:33.716 align:middle
for the-big changes in
rates to matter a lot

44:33.716 --> 44:34.676 align:middle
for the housing sector.

44:35.296 --> 44:37.196 align:middle
And, you know, the other
thing is by-you know,

44:37.196 --> 44:39.476 align:middle
by achieving maximum
employment and price stability,

44:39.676 --> 44:40.706 align:middle
that's a strong economy.

44:40.706 --> 44:42.986 align:middle
That's a good economy
for housing.

44:43.226 --> 44:46.416 align:middle
And then the last thing
I'll say is, you know,

44:46.416 --> 44:48.976 align:middle
there's a deeper problem here
that is not a cyclical problem

44:48.976 --> 44:49.966 align:middle
that the Fed can address,

44:49.966 --> 44:53.426 align:middle
and that just is a pretty much
nationwide housing shortage,

44:53.906 --> 44:55.246 align:middle
or a lot of places

44:55.246 --> 44:58.976 align:middle
in the country just don't have
enough housing for people.

44:59.256 --> 45:01.596 align:middle
And, you know, all of the areas

45:01.596 --> 45:03.366 align:middle
around metropolitan
areas-like Washington,

45:03.366 --> 45:05.926 align:middle
for example-are very built
up, and so you're having

45:05.926 --> 45:07.236 align:middle
to build farther
and farther out.

45:07.236 --> 45:09.936 align:middle
And that-so that's where it is.

45:10.476 --> 45:11.416 align:middle
NICOLE GOODKIND.

45:11.416 --> 45:12.386 align:middle
And just a quick follow-up.

45:12.546 --> 45:14.076 align:middle
During the last SEP,

45:14.076 --> 45:15.726 align:middle
or the press conference
following the last SEP,

45:15.726 --> 45:19.126 align:middle
you seemed to indicate that
policymakers lacked conviction

45:19.426 --> 45:21.186 align:middle
about their projections.

45:21.186 --> 45:22.676 align:middle
And I'm wondering if
you still feel that way.

45:22.956 --> 45:23.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:24.746 --> 45:29.356 align:middle
You know, forecasting is very
difficult, even in placid times.

45:29.546 --> 45:33.896 align:middle
And, as I've mentioned before,
forecasters are a humble lot,

45:33.896 --> 45:35.096 align:middle
with much to be humble about.

45:35.876 --> 45:39.546 align:middle
So I think right now is a
particularly challenging time,

45:39.686 --> 45:41.296 align:middle
even more uncertain than usual.

45:41.766 --> 45:46.386 align:middle
And so I don't know any
forecaster anywhere really-ask

45:46.386 --> 45:49.856 align:middle
any of the forecasters whether
they have great confidence

45:49.856 --> 45:50.826 align:middle
in their forecast right now.

45:50.826 --> 45:52.446 align:middle
I think they'll honestly
say "no."

45:55.156 --> 45:57.466 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

45:57.876 --> 45:58.716 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

45:58.716 --> 46:00.856 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

46:00.936 --> 46:04.666 align:middle
If you're cutting rates, why
continue to reduce the size

46:04.666 --> 46:07.176 align:middle
of your balance sheet [rather]
than pause the unwinding?

46:08.126 --> 46:09.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:09.286 --> 46:13.156 align:middle
Well, I think we're-you
know, we're cutting the size

46:13.156 --> 46:14.916 align:middle
of our balance sheet
quite marginally now.

46:14.916 --> 46:16.156 align:middle
As you know, with
the balance sheet,

46:17.336 --> 46:20.956 align:middle
we're still in an
abundant-reserves condition.

46:21.326 --> 46:27.246 align:middle
And we've said that we would
stop somewhat above a-somewhat

46:27.246 --> 46:29.326 align:middle
above an ample-reserves level.

46:29.436 --> 46:30.496 align:middle
That's where we are.

46:30.496 --> 46:31.996 align:middle
And we're-you know, we're
getting closer to that.

46:31.996 --> 46:33.296 align:middle
We're monitoring
it very carefully.

46:33.526 --> 46:35.456 align:middle
We don't think that
that has, at all,

46:35.736 --> 46:37.976 align:middle
significant macroeconomic
effects.

46:37.976 --> 46:40.116 align:middle
These are pretty
small numbers moving

46:40.116 --> 46:42.016 align:middle
around inside a giant economy.

46:42.016 --> 46:45.466 align:middle
You know, the level of
runoff is not very large.

46:45.466 --> 46:48.246 align:middle
So I wouldn't attribute
macroeconomic consequences

46:48.316 --> 46:49.076 align:middle
to that, at this point.

46:49.166 --> 46:49.836 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:49.836 --> 46:53.026 align:middle
And at his recent confirmation
hearing, Stephen Miran brought

46:53.026 --> 46:55.406 align:middle
up that the Fed actually
has three mandates

46:55.406 --> 46:58.196 align:middle
from Congress-not just
jobs and stable prices,

46:58.196 --> 47:00.736 align:middle
but also moderate
long-term interest rates.

47:01.096 --> 47:02.836 align:middle
So what does Congress mean

47:02.836 --> 47:04.576 align:middle
by "moderate long-term
interest rates?"

47:04.576 --> 47:06.026 align:middle
How should we understand

47:06.026 --> 47:08.586 align:middle
that when we see the
10-year Treasury moving?

47:08.586 --> 47:11.186 align:middle
And how do you think about
this part of the mandate

47:11.386 --> 47:13.186 align:middle
when policy choices,
like rate cuts

47:13.186 --> 47:14.396 align:middle
or balance sheet reductions,

47:14.396 --> 47:15.866 align:middle
affect the long end
of the yield curve?

47:18.016 --> 47:18.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:18.626 --> 47:22.866 align:middle
So we always think of it as the
dual mandate, maximum employment

47:22.866 --> 47:26.016 align:middle
and price stability, for a long
time, because we think moderate,

47:26.016 --> 47:28.576 align:middle
long-term interest rates are
something that will result

47:28.916 --> 47:32.566 align:middle
from stable inflation-low
and stable inflation

47:32.996 --> 47:34.556 align:middle
and maximum employment.

47:34.646 --> 47:39.836 align:middle
So we don't-we haven't thought
about that for a very long time

47:39.836 --> 47:43.686 align:middle
as a third mandate that
requires independent action.

47:43.966 --> 47:45.566 align:middle
So that's where that is.

47:46.566 --> 47:50.046 align:middle
And there's no thought
of-as far as I'm concerned,

47:50.046 --> 47:53.086 align:middle
there's no thought of
considering that, you know,

47:53.776 --> 47:56.446 align:middle
considering that we
somehow incorporate that in

47:56.446 --> 47:57.926 align:middle
as something in a different way.

47:58.516 --> 47:59.376 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

48:01.616 --> 48:04.466 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

48:04.466 --> 48:06.606 align:middle
Matt Egan from CNN.

48:06.746 --> 48:10.646 align:middle
We recently learned that
average FICO credit scores are

48:10.786 --> 48:12.106 align:middle
down this year by 2 points,

48:12.106 --> 48:14.726 align:middle
the most since 2009
during the Great Recession.

48:14.726 --> 48:16.916 align:middle
And delinquencies are
high for car loans,

48:16.916 --> 48:18.216 align:middle
personal loans, credit cards.

48:18.816 --> 48:20.286 align:middle
How concerned, if at all,

48:20.286 --> 48:23.466 align:middle
are you about the health
of consumer finances?

48:23.466 --> 48:26.166 align:middle
And do you expect
today's cut will help?

48:26.166 --> 48:26.786 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:27.456 --> 48:29.636 align:middle
So, you know, we're
aware of that.

48:29.696 --> 48:33.076 align:middle
I think default rates have
been kind of ticking up.

48:33.076 --> 48:34.116 align:middle
And we do watch that.

48:34.736 --> 48:37.576 align:middle
They're not at a level-I
don't believe they're

48:37.576 --> 48:39.276 align:middle
at a level where,
overall, they're,

48:39.356 --> 48:41.106 align:middle
you know, terribly concerning.

48:41.106 --> 48:42.436 align:middle
But it is something
that we watch.

48:42.916 --> 48:45.806 align:middle
You know, lower rates should
support economic activity.

48:45.806 --> 48:47.986 align:middle
I don't know that one rate
cut will have, you know,

48:47.986 --> 48:49.096 align:middle
a visible effect on that.

48:49.156 --> 48:51.636 align:middle
But over time, you
know, a strong economy

48:51.636 --> 48:55.126 align:middle
with a strong labor market is
what we're-what we're aiming

48:55.126 --> 48:56.956 align:middle
for, and stable prices.

48:56.956 --> 48:58.326 align:middle
So that should help.

48:58.326 --> 48:59.126 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

48:59.206 --> 49:00.116 align:middle
Just a follow-up.

49:00.176 --> 49:04.906 align:middle
This rate cut is coming at a
time when the stock market is at

49:04.906 --> 49:06.206 align:middle
or near all-time highs.

49:06.206 --> 49:09.646 align:middle
And some valuation metrics
are elevated historically.

49:10.086 --> 49:13.556 align:middle
Is there a risk that cutting
rates could overheat financial

49:13.556 --> 49:15.516 align:middle
markets, potentially
fueling a bubble?

49:15.516 --> 49:17.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:17.266 --> 49:20.256 align:middle
You know, we're tightly
focused on our goals, right?

49:20.356 --> 49:23.646 align:middle
And our goals are maximum
employment and price stability.

49:23.646 --> 49:26.956 align:middle
And so we take the
actions that we take

49:27.416 --> 49:29.196 align:middle
with an eye on those goals.

49:29.926 --> 49:33.046 align:middle
Separately-and that's why
we did what we did today.

49:33.046 --> 49:36.426 align:middle
Separately, we monitor financial
stability very, very carefully.

49:36.896 --> 49:40.206 align:middle
And, you know, I would
say it's a mixed picture,

49:40.206 --> 49:42.726 align:middle
but households are in good
shape, banks are in good shape.

49:42.956 --> 49:47.046 align:middle
Overall, households are still
in good shape in the aggregate.

49:47.046 --> 49:48.616 align:middle
And I know that people
at the lower end

49:48.616 --> 49:50.846 align:middle
of the income spectrum are
under pressure, obviously.

49:50.916 --> 49:53.686 align:middle
But from a financial
stability perspective,

49:53.686 --> 49:54.856 align:middle
we monitor that picture.

49:55.426 --> 49:58.086 align:middle
We don't have a view that
there's a right or wrong level

49:58.086 --> 50:01.076 align:middle
of asset prices for any
particular financial asset,

50:01.076 --> 50:05.386 align:middle
but we monitor the whole
picture, really looking

50:05.386 --> 50:07.326 align:middle
for structural vulnerabilities,

50:07.896 --> 50:12.376 align:middle
and I would say those are
not elevated right now.

50:12.616 --> 50:13.606 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

50:15.536 --> 50:17.216 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

50:17.256 --> 50:18.986 align:middle
Jean Yung with MNI Market News.

50:19.146 --> 50:22.316 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
inflation expectations.

50:23.406 --> 50:25.486 align:middle
You've said the Fed
can't take the stability

50:25.486 --> 50:27.546 align:middle
of inflation expectations
for granted.

50:28.116 --> 50:30.846 align:middle
You mentioned at the short run
they've gone up a little bit.

50:30.846 --> 50:32.666 align:middle
I wonder if you can
talk a bit about that.

50:33.196 --> 50:36.236 align:middle
And then also at the
long run, I'm wondering,

50:36.236 --> 50:40.026 align:middle
do you see evidence that the
debate over Fed independence

50:40.026 --> 50:42.706 align:middle
and the growing deficit
is putting pressure

50:42.706 --> 50:44.176 align:middle
on inflation expectations?

50:44.176 --> 50:45.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:45.566 --> 50:49.986 align:middle
So, as you said, shorter-term
inflation expectations have

50:49.986 --> 50:52.466 align:middle
tended to respond to
near-term inflation,

50:52.466 --> 50:53.596 align:middle
so if inflation goes up,

50:54.546 --> 50:56.516 align:middle
inflation expectations
will predict

50:56.516 --> 50:58.166 align:middle
that it takes just a little
while to get back down.

50:58.166 --> 51:01.836 align:middle
Fortunately, throughout
this period,

51:02.276 --> 51:05.206 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations, both breakeven

51:05.206 --> 51:06.836 align:middle
in the markets and almost all

51:06.836 --> 51:09.226 align:middle
of the longer-term
surveys-Michigan being a bit

51:09.226 --> 51:14.436 align:middle
of an outlier lately-have been
just rock solid in terms of,

51:14.736 --> 51:18.356 align:middle
you know, running at
levels that are consistent

51:18.356 --> 51:19.866 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation
over time.

51:19.866 --> 51:21.946 align:middle
So we don't take
that for granted.

51:22.066 --> 51:25.586 align:middle
We actually assume that our
actions have a real effect

51:25.586 --> 51:28.806 align:middle
on that, and that, you
know, we need to, you know,

51:28.806 --> 51:33.726 align:middle
continually show and also
mention/discuss our commitment

51:33.726 --> 51:35.526 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation.

51:35.646 --> 51:37.736 align:middle
And so you'll hear
us doing that.

51:37.736 --> 51:41.076 align:middle
But, you know, we're-as
I mentioned,

51:41.136 --> 51:44.156 align:middle
it's a difficult situation
because we have risks

51:44.156 --> 51:47.646 align:middle
that are both affecting the
labor market and inflation.

51:48.136 --> 51:49.376 align:middle
Those are our two goals.

51:49.376 --> 51:50.996 align:middle
And so we have to
balance those two.

51:50.996 --> 51:52.656 align:middle
When they're both at risk,
we have to balance them,

51:52.656 --> 51:53.946 align:middle
and that's really what
we're trying to do.

51:54.136 --> 51:59.856 align:middle
I don't- the latter part of your
question was about independence.

51:59.856 --> 52:04.216 align:middle
You know, I don't see market
participants-I don't see

52:04.216 --> 52:06.166 align:middle
that that's something they're
factoring in right now in terms

52:06.166 --> 52:07.996 align:middle
of setting interest rates.

52:08.676 --> 52:10.096 align:middle
Thanks very much.

